WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.230
Hi it's me Michelle from Lab Muffin
Beauty Science. I'm a chemistry PhD

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:08.069
graduate who loves skincare science, and
today I'm going to be telling you about

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:15.660
how miceller water works. Micellar water
is a type of cleanser that's super

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.279
popular with makeup artists and beauty
addicts. The most famous micellar water

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:25.050
is probably Bioderma Sensibio H2O.
Apparently one bottle of this is sold

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:30.359
every five seconds worldwide. A quick
chemistry side note: it's H2O,

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:35.700
as in the letter O, not the number zero.
This is a pet peeve of mine as a chemist

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.590
because H2O is the molecular
formula for water. What this means is

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:45.660
that there are 2 hydrogen atoms
bonded to 1 oxygen atom. So we have two

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:51.090
Hs and one O. H20 would mean that
there are 20 hydrogens bonded together,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:57.750
which just doesn't happen, except maybe
once in a star, somewhere. Anyway, back to

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.739
micellar water! Micellar waters are
cleansers that you pour into a cotton

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:06.540
pad, then wipe over your skin. You don't
need to rinse them after you use them, so

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.640
it's almost like makeup wipe liquid in a
bottle. How do they work? We're going to

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.939
have to talk about the physical
chemistry of surfactants to understand

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:22.890
what's going on. The key ingredients in
micellar water are a surfactant or

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:28.740
multiple surfactants, and water. Oil and
water usually repel each other and oil

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.329
won't dissolve in water or vice versa,
which is why you can't wash an oily dish

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:38.369
with plain water. This is because oil is
non-polar and water is polar.

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:43.310
It's like oil is velcro but water is a
magnet: they just don't work together.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:47.610
This is a chemical principle that's
commonly known as "like dissolves like",

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:52.439
which means that oil and water don't
form sticky interactions with each other.

00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:56.280
But if you add surfactants to the mix,
that's a different story.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.259
Surfactants are nifty little molecules
that have two halves: a hydrophilic head

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:06.390
that's attracted to water and repels oil,
and a hydrophobic or lipophilic half

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:10.110
that's attracted to oils and grease and
repels water.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.850
It's like you've sticky taped a magnet
to velcro. Now everything can stick

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:19.800
together in a big happy heap. In
chemistry this happy mixture is called

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.540
an emulsion. Because getting oil and
water to hang out together happily is

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.750
really handy, you'll find surfactants in
lots of products: soaps, shampoos,

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:36.480
dishwashing detergent, laundry powders,
moisturisers, foundations, mayonnaise, milk,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.960
ice cream, and of course, micellar water.
For example with an oily dish, the

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.620
surfactants in the detergent can sink
into the oil. Then when you scrub the

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.660
dish, the surfactants break up the oil
into droplets. Since the surfactants hang

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:56.100
out at the surface between oil and water,
they sort of hide the oil from the water.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:02.610
It's like it smuggles the oil into the
water so you can wash it away. It's the

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:07.110
same idea with mayonnaise. Oil and
vinegar usually don't mix, but if you

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:11.880
have lecithin from egg yolk as an
emulsifier or surfactant, it can smuggle

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.380
in droplets of oil into the vinegar, and
they stay together as a thick creamy

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.239
delicious goop. When you use micellar
water, you pour the product onto a cotton

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:24.810
pad. Cotton is made of cellulose which is
polar,

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.739
so the hydrophilic heads are going to
stick to the cotton like magnets and the

00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:32.820
hydrophobic tails are going to stick out
on the surface of the pad like velcro.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:38.400
And guess what? Make up and oils and
grease are non-polar, so they're like

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.140
velcro as well. So when you wipe the pad
over your skin, the tails will grab the

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:50.459
makeup and oils and grease so you end up with clean skin. You might be wondering

00:03:50.459 --> 00:03:55.830
when I'm going to start talking about micelles. Micelles aren't actually that

00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:01.019
important to how micellar water works.
When you add a surfactant to water,

00:04:01.019 --> 00:04:05.220
like if you mix dishwashing detergent
with tap water, we have the hydrophilic

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.780
heads and the hydrophobic tails of the
surfactants and a bunch of water. Since

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.100
the hydrophobic tails don't like water
and the hydrophilic heads do, the

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:18.579
surfactant molecules are going to
arrange themselves or self-assemble so

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:23.830
the tails aren't touching the water.
They can do this in two ways. The tails

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:28.000
can stick out of the water into the air
at the surface, sort of like ostriches

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.289
burying their heads into the sand. Or
they can arrange themselves into little

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:36.159
balls with the heads at the surface and
the tails on the inside, sort of like

00:04:36.159 --> 00:04:42.069
tiny pin cushions. These little balls are
called micelles. Any surfactant in water

00:04:42.069 --> 00:04:49.419
will form micelles. The main difference
with micellar water is that it's

00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:52.810
formulated so that the surfactants
aren't as damaging to your skin as a

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:57.699
standard rinse off cleanser.
Unfortunately since surfactants are sticky

00:04:57.699 --> 00:05:02.409
they tend to stick to things in
your skin such as proteins, which changes

00:05:02.409 --> 00:05:07.180
the structure of your skin. Since your
skin's main role is to act as a barrier

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:12.310
to keep water in and unwanted germs and
chemicals out, this means that you can

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:17.349
end up with a leaky barrier. Micellar
waters are made so that the surfactants

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:22.449
won't do this as much as a standard
rinse off cleanser. For example they tend

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:26.259
to contain gentler surfactants than a
regular cleanser. They're also sometimes

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:31.300
formulated with moisturizing ingredients
like fatty acid esters to keep the skin

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:36.580
barrier intact. But for some people who
have sensitive skin, even micellar water

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:42.009
might be too much. So if you find that
your skin is dry or itchy or tight after

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:46.449
you use micellar water, you can try
wiping it off or rinsing it off with a

00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:51.190
wet towel or just water. So that's how micellar water works! I hope you found this

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:55.330
video useful. If you liked it please
subscribe to my channel, check out my

00:05:55.330 --> 00:06:03.310
blog, follow me on Instagram, send me cake, send me science memes and I'll see you

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.750
next time for more beauty science :)

